const underwaterSound: ExamListeningSource = {
  part: 4, title: 'Separating biological sounds from harbour noise', topic: 'Marine science and environmental monitoring', band: 7.5, style: 'diagram',
  speech: [
    ['Lecturer', 'Underwater acoustic surveys can reveal activity that would be difficult to observe from the shore. Nevertheless, an acoustic record is not a transparent account of everything happening beneath the surface. Sound travels differently as conditions change, and a loud event can conceal a quieter one. The coastal study I will describe was designed to examine those limitations while monitoring biological activity near a working harbour. Its researchers wanted a method useful for management without claiming an impossible level of certainty.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'The principal study area was a sheltered inlet. A site directly beside the shipping channel would have produced abundant recordings but very few opportunities to distinguish quieter biological signals. The inlet still experienced harbour traffic, yet it also contained periods when boats were absent. That variation allowed the researchers to compare conditions rather than simply catalogue noise. They obtained permission for fixed equipment and avoided attaching it to infrastructure where routine maintenance could alter its position without warning.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'Recording began after a calibration signal. A device emitted a sound of known characteristics near the instrument, allowing the team to check whether the system responded consistently. This preliminary stage did not identify every later source, but it provided a baseline against which equipment changes could be recognised. Without calibration, a weaker recording might be interpreted as reduced animal activity when the true cause was a technical problem, a damaged connector or an altered instrument setting.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'The team recorded water temperature throughout the survey. Temperature affects sound transmission, and a comparison between warm and cool periods can otherwise be misleading. Researchers also noted tide height, but temperature was the variable added to the interpretation model first. They did not attempt to correct the recordings with a single universal formula. Local depth and the shape of the inlet influenced propagation, so the model was checked against observations rather than treated as an unquestionable physical shortcut.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'The clearest biological pattern appeared during dusk periods. Repeated bursts were less evident in the middle of the day, when boat engines dominated many recordings. This finding suggested a useful monitoring window but did not prove that animals were inactive at other times. A signal may be absent from a recording because it is masked or because its source is farther away. The distinction between absence of evidence and evidence of absence is especially important in this type of survey.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'Engine noise was identified partly from harbour logs. Staff recorded departures and arrivals, and the researchers compared those times with prominent low-frequency events. The logs were not perfect: a small private boat could pass without appearing on them. Still, they provided an independent check that improved interpretation. Automatically classifying every low-frequency sound as a vessel would have been too crude, since some natural processes also contribute energy within that range, particularly during rough weather or rapid water movement.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'To reduce the risk of mistaking isolated interference for a pattern, the analysis required repeated detections. A single brief sound could be retained in the archive without being used as evidence of a recurring biological event. Researchers compared several evenings and examined whether similar signals appeared under comparable conditions. This conservative rule reduced dramatic claims based on unusual clips. It also meant that rare events might be underrepresented, a trade-off the final report made explicit rather than hiding.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'Now look at the simplified apparatus diagram. The upper component is the hydrophone. It converts pressure changes in the water into an electrical signal and sits clear of the seabed so sediment does not obstruct its housing. Below it, the lower component is the weighted anchor. A short tether connects the two. The anchor maintains position, but the instrument is not rigidly fixed to a harbour wall, where vibration from nearby machinery could enter the recording through the structure.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'The main operational problem was cable movement. During stronger currents, a loose section touched the housing and produced a scratching sound. At first that sounded like a new environmental event, but its close relationship with current speed and its abrupt disappearance after the cable was secured revealed the cause. This illustrates why inspecting equipment is part of interpretation, not merely a maintenance task. A sophisticated classification model cannot rescue a recording if the physical origin of an artefact is misunderstood.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'Recordings were stored in a shared archive. Each file was accompanied by equipment settings, environmental readings and notes on unusual events, so another researcher could examine a conclusion rather than accept a labelled sound on trust. The archive excluded conversations recorded accidentally at the surface. Access was controlled, and public examples were carefully selected. Open scientific scrutiny does not require releasing every raw file when some contain information unrelated to the ecological question and potentially sensitive to other people.'],
    ['Lecturer', 'The next stage will use paired stations. Comparing synchronised records from two positions may help distinguish an event occurring throughout the inlet from interference local to one instrument. The team is not proposing that every harbour install a network immediately. First it must establish what additional information the second station provides, and whether that gain justifies the cost of equipment and analysis.'],
  ],
  facts: [
    ['Principal study area', 'sheltered inlet', 'The principal study area was a sheltered inlet.'],
    ['Signal used before the main recording began', 'calibration signal', 'Recording began after a calibration signal.'],
    ['Environmental variable added first to the interpretation model', 'water temperature', 'The team recorded water temperature throughout the survey.'],
    ['Time associated with the clearest biological pattern', 'dusk periods', 'The clearest biological pattern appeared during dusk periods.'],
    ['Independent records used to help identify engine noise', 'harbour logs', 'Engine noise was identified partly from harbour logs.'],
    ['Requirement before an event counted as a recurring pattern', 'repeated detections', 'the analysis required repeated detections.'],
    ['Upper component of the apparatus', 'hydrophone', 'The upper component is the hydrophone.'],
    ['Lower component of the apparatus', 'weighted anchor', 'the lower component is the weighted anchor.'],
    ['Main operational problem', 'cable movement', 'The main operational problem was cable movement.'],
    ['Storage system used for recordings and their associated metadata', 'shared archive', 'Recordings were stored in a shared archive.'],
  ],
};

